Description: Look up the service access group without a trailing newline
 When a user name has no UUID, pam_sacl looks up the service access group
 to see whether access to the service is limited. The name it looked up
 ended in a newline, so the group was never found. The user was then let in
 unless com.apple.access_all_services existed.
Author: The Blueberry Pie OS Authors
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-17

--- a/modules/pam_sacl/pam_sacl.c
+++ b/modules/pam_sacl/pam_sacl.c
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
  */
 /*
  * Modified 2026-11-11: a trust account is one with no passwd entry (APSL 2.0 section 2.2(b)).
+ * Modified 2026-11-17: the service access group name has no newline (APSL 2.0 section 2.2(b)).
  */
 
 #include <stdio.h>
@@ -111,7 +112,7 @@ PAM_EXTERN int pam_sm_acct_mgmt(pam_hand
 		 * this if the relevant SACL groups exist.
 		 */
 
-		if (asprintf(&sacl_group, "com.apple.access_%s\n",
+		if (asprintf(&sacl_group, "com.apple.access_%s",
 							service) == -1) {
 			return PAM_SYSTEM_ERR;
 		}
